Changes   Established by comparing these files with the upstream data. The tool that made the
          conversion is not known.
          - Converted from upstream YAML to this app's object-set JSON format.
          - Positions converted from pixels on the 8192 x 8192 map layer, measured from its
            bottom-left corner, to percentages measured from the top-left corner:
            x = x_px / 8192 * 100 and y = 100 - y_px / 8192 * 100, rounded to two decimals.
          - Each marker's region became its description, its subtype became its category, and
            its title was set to the name of that category.
          - Marker ids gained an "oss-" prefix.
          - The name field (a number on Empyrean Traces, a Chinese place name on teleports and
            other locations, often empty), images and the per-marker category were dropped.
            A marker's upstream category now shows only as the parent of its subtype in the
            category tree.
          - Categories were given new ids, names and colours (upstream monolithMaterial is
            "Empyrean Trace" here). The nested subtypes became parentId links; order,
            canComplete, hideTooltip and iconScale were dropped; icon values were replaced
            (empty, except on Empyrean Trace); visible was added, plus iconWidth and iconHeight
            on Empyrean Trace.
          - mapName and backgroundImage keys were added at the top level.
          - The icon and backgroundImage paths point to files this repository does not contain.
          For the gathering points, made by scripts/import_gathering.py:
          - Converted from upstream JSON to the same object-set format. Positions converted from
            pixels on the 8192 x 8192 map layer, from its top-left corner, to percentages:
            x = x_px / 8192 * 100 and y = y_px / 8192 * 100, rounded to three decimals.
          - Each upstream gathering subtype became a category gathering-<name> under a new
            "gathering" category, named and titled by its English name; its colour was added.
          - Marker ids gained an "oss-" prefix; z, images, contributors, indexInSubtype and tier
            were dropped, and the description left empty.
          For the hidden cubes of altgard.json, made by scripts/import_cubes.py:
          - The upstream cubes were replaced by every spot a cube can appear at, from a list of
            the game's cube groups in world coordinates, which is not upstream data.
          - Each spot's "level" (up or down) compares its height with the median z of the
            upstream markers within 100 pixels of it, or of its own group where fewer than
            three are near.


assets/maps/altgard/reference.webp
assets/maps/altgard/detail.webp
assets/maps/verteron/reference.webp
assets/maps/verteron/detail.webp
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Source    The map layer images of the aion2-interactive-map project by tc-imba:
          https://github.com/aion2-interactive-map/aion2-interactive-map
          altgard/ holds the World_D_A layer (Asmodae) and verteron/ the World_L_A layer
          (Elysea). Each reference.webp is the 4096 x 4096 overview the project serves,
          retrieved on 2026-09-23. altgard/detail.webp is the same World_D_A layer at
          8192 x 8192, retrieved on 2026-09-19. verteron/detail.webp is the World_L_A layer at
          8192 x 8192, joined from the 64 tiles under public/UI/Map/WorldMap/World_L_A/Res/ in
          the project's repository at commit 89ce398 (2026-04-22), retrieved on 2026-09-24. The
          manifest.json beside each pair of images repeats this.

Licence   Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial 4.0 International (CC BY-NC 4.0),
          https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/4.0/
          on the same terms, and with the same links, as the object sets above.

Changes   The reference.webp files and altgard/detail.webp are used byte for byte as retrieved.
          verteron/detail.webp was made by placing the 64 tiles of 1024 x 1024 in their 8 x 8
          grid (file names give column, then row) and saving the result as one WebP at quality
          90. The app cuts each detail.webp into a tile pyramid, and each reference.webp into a
          thumbnail, at run time in the user's data directory; nothing derived from them is in
          this repository.
